Source: b1nix-meta
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: b1nix maintainers <maintainers@b1nix.invalid>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13)
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/B1nix/b1nix
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: b1nix-base-files
Architecture: all
Priority: required
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: b1nix system identification and apt configuration
 What turns an installed Debian into b1nix: /etc/os-release, the apt source for
 the b1nix overlay and its signing key, and the apt preference that keeps
 Debian's own linux-image packages from being pulled in by a dependency.
 .
 The overlay's URL lives here and nowhere else, so that moving it later is a
 package update rather than an instruction every user has to follow by hand.

Package: b1nix-desktop
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         plasma-desktop,
         plasma-workspace-wayland,
         sddm,
         pipewire,
         pipewire-pulse,
         wireplumber,
         network-manager,
         plasma-nm,
         xdg-desktop-portal-kde,
         konsole,
         fonts-dejavu,
         fonts-noto-core,
         fonts-noto-color-emoji,
         b1nix-base-files
Recommends: dolphin,
            kate,
            firefox-esr,
            gwenview,
            ark,
            spectacle,
            plasma-systemmonitor,
            cups,
            system-config-printer
Suggests: chromium, libreoffice, kdenlive
Description: b1nix desktop (metapackage)
 Plasma on Wayland with the applications a desktop needs and nothing more.
 This is a dependency list, not a collection of files.
 .
 Depends is what a desktop cannot boot without; Recommends is expected but
 removable, and the system still works when it is gone; Suggests is named so
 that it can be found, and is never installed by default.

Package: b1nix-tools
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, pciutils, usbutils
Description: diagnostic tools for b1nix
 b1nix-report collects everything a bug report needs -- release, kernel and its
 build id, CPU, PCI and USB identifiers, which drivers bound, failed units and
 the boot log -- into one plain-text file that the reporter can read before
 sending it.
 .
 The same file feeds the hardware compatibility list, so its header format is
 stable across releases.
